 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S441-004\", \"clauses\": {\"organize_data\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F05\", \"template\": \"Count the pictures in each row of the {pg_word}, then write the counts for {cat_list} in the {table_word}.\", \"reasoning\": \"F05 asks the learner to count the pictures in each row and write the counts into the table, which is the organizing task. Its cat_list slot comes from F09, whose members are the table's categories.\"}, \"pictograph\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F07\", \"template\": \"The {pg_word} shows {cat_list}. Fill in the {table_word} with the correct counts.\", \"reasoning\": \"pg_word is drawn from F01 or F08, which both print the word pictograph, so F07 names the object in the question text.\"}, \"without_scale\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F02\", \"template\": \"Each picture stands for 1 item.\", \"reasoning\": \"F02 is a fixed hint stating a one-to-one value for each picture, which is the no-scale property. No family prints the word scale, which is in NOT_YET_KNOWN, and none needs to.\"}, \"table\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F06\", \"template\": \"Look at the {pg_word}. Write the correct count for each row in the {table_word}: {cat_list}.\", \"reasoning\": \"table_word is gated to the word table, which is in student_vocab and introduces_vocab, so F06 names the table as the representation to write into.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F09\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"{join} and {v}\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"F09 is the cat_list family, printed nested inside F05, F06 and F07. Its join slot is F10, whose p0, p1 and p2 slots are declared distinct_in_every_render: true. Its v slot is a separate domain, 'one of the generator's own choices' with basis 'the table's categories', and nothing in the packet excludes the values drawn for p0, p1 or p2. So F09 can print the same category twice in one cat_list. F05, F06 and F07 then ask the learner to write a count for each named category in the table, so a duplicated category gives the table a repeated row with two counts for one category. A learner cannot tell which count is right. That is a misleading statement under Rule 1, and the failing family is F09, with template {join} and {v}. Not counted as a fault: the domain members mix kinds (days, subjects, fruit, colours, grade labels, activities) in one shared set. Each printed sentence is still a true statement about the table, and the vocabulary used is within cumulative_vocab or plain English. No printed word is in NOT_YET_KNOWN. F02 and F04 use only plain words and the gated word table.\"}",
